WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.775
Report-only is a policy state,
not a test environment.

2
00:00:03.775 --> 00:00:08.340
The policy is evaluated at every
sign-in and its result is logged,

3
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.062
but nothing is enforced.

4
00:00:10.062 --> 00:00:13.362
There are four results, and you'll
see them in the sign-in logs

5
00:00:13.362 --> 00:00:16.662
and in Microsoft Graph under
the names in the middle column.

6
00:00:16.662 --> 00:00:21.831
Success means the conditions matched
and the controls were already satisfied,

7
00:00:21.831 --> 00:00:24.963
for example an MFA claim
was already in the token.

8
00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:28.506
Failure means the conditions
matched and a non-interactive

9
00:00:28.506 --> 00:00:30.987
control failed: that's
what a block would do.

10
00:00:30.987 --> 00:00:34.906
User action required, which Graph
calls report-only interrupted,

11
00:00:34.906 --> 00:00:37.300
means the user would have been prompted.

12
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.383
And not applied means the
conditions didn't match,

13
00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:42.563
often because the user is excluded.

14
00:00:42.563 --> 00:00:46.988
Two limits: user-action policies
can't run in report-only at all,

15
00:00:46.988 --> 00:00:51.500
and GPS-based named locations
still prompt users for location.

16
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.319
To change an enforced policy, Microsoft's
practice is to create a report-only

17
00:00:56.319 --> 00:01:00.450
copy with the change, compare the
two, and then update the original.

18
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:04.150
Rollback has four forms,
from smallest to largest.

19
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.575
Revert one policy to report-only when
you need data while you investigate.

20
00:01:08.575 --> 00:01:13.511
Reduce scope with a time-bound exclusion
group when one population is blocked,

21
00:01:13.511 --> 00:01:15.713
fix the cause, then remove the group.

22
00:01:15.713 --> 00:01:19.113
Turn a policy Off when it's
causing widespread blocks.

23
00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:22.506
And when several policies
in a wave interact badly,

24
00:01:22.506 --> 00:01:26.062
set the whole wave to
report-only and open an incident.

25
00:01:26.062 --> 00:01:28.350
What's never on the list is deletion.

26
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:33.285
Microsoft does let you restore a deleted
policy or named location within thirty

27
00:01:33.285 --> 00:01:37.704
days, but a deletion breaks the policy's
change history in source control,

28
00:01:37.704 --> 00:01:39.987
and the drift record has to explain it.

29
00:01:39.987 --> 00:01:42.317
If the tenant is locked out entirely,

30
00:01:42.317 --> 00:01:45.812
an emergency account signs
in and reverses the change.

31
00:01:45.812 --> 00:01:47.325
If no administrator can,

32
00:01:47.325 --> 00:01:51.840
Microsoft support can update the
blocking policy after verification.
